{"from": "site:mount-tabor", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-01-high-mountain", "grade": "D", "note": "Mount Tabor 'is traditionally identified as the Mount of Transfiguration'; 'since the 3rd century, some Christians have identified Mount Tabor as the site of the transfiguration, including Origen'. A tradition of identification, not one that can be secured - and the article itself records the fortress objection."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-4-tabor", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:mt-17-01-high-mountain", "note": "Josephus: in the war of 66-67 CE the band 'that had seized upon Mount Tabor' held a summit whose 'top is a plain of twenty-six furlongs, and all encompassed with a wall'; Placidus took it by cutting off their water. A walled, inhabited top complicates the secluded-mountain setting (the Tabor tradition answers that this does not rule out the summit)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sukkah-1-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-04-three-tents", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Sukkah rules the sukka's roofing and walls unfit over twenty cubits or under ten handbreadths - the booth (sukka) that Jews dwell in at Tabernacles; the custom behind Peter's 'three tents' (redacted c. 200 CE, describing Second Temple practice)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-eduyot-8-7", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-10-13-elijah-first", "grade": "C", "note": "Rabbinic tradition expects Elijah's return before the day of the Lord ('Behold I send to you Elijah the prophet...') - the scribes' teaching that 'Elijah must come first' (reused from ch 11). Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:malachi-4-5", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-17-10-13-elijah-first", "note": "Cross-reference: Malachi 4:5 (WEB) 'Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the great and terrible day of Yahweh comes' - the scriptural basis of the expectation. A biblical text is not independent evidence."}
{"from": "text:hippocrates-sacred-disease", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-15-moonstruck-boy", "grade": "C", "note": "Hippocratic On the Sacred Disease (c. 400 BCE) opens: epilepsy 'appears to me to be nowise more divine nor more sacred than other diseases' - written against the standing view that the 'sacred disease' is divine; the ancient category behind the boy's condition."}
{"from": "observation:mustard-botany", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-20-mustard-seed-faith", "grade": "C", "note": "Black mustard is an annual herb grown for its tiny seeds - the proverbial smallness of the 'grain of mustard seed' (reused from ch 13; the mountain-moving is hyperbole, not evidence)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-shekalim-1-3", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-24-temple-tax", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Shekalim 1:3: 'On the first of Adar the court proclaims concerning the collection of shekels, i.e., the yearly half-shekel contribution to the Temple treasury made by each adult male' - the specific tax institution confirmed (redacted c. 200 CE, describing Second Temple practice)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-312", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-24-temple-tax", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: diaspora Jews deposited 'that half shekel which every one, by the custom of our country, offers unto God' in treasury cities, whence 'they were transmitted to Jerusalem' - a first-century text naming the tax itself, en route in the 30s CE (Ant. 18.312 by the brief's numbering; the Wikisource page does not print section numbers)."}
{"from": "artifact:tyrian-shekel", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-24-temple-tax", "grade": "B", "note": "Tyrian shekels' 'exceptional purity helps explain why the Jerusalem Temple priests specifically required Tyrian shekels for Temple tax payments' (reused from ch 06) - the required payment coin of the very tax the claim is about."}
{"from": "site:capernaum", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-24-temple-tax", "grade": "B", "note": "Capernaum is excavated and was established in the 2nd century BC as a Hasmonean fishing village - the real lakeside town where the didrachma collectors meet Peter."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-24-temple-tax", "grade": "C", "note": "The Second Temple served as the chief place of worship and drew pilgrims for the Three Pilgrimage Festivals - the institution whose upkeep the annual half-shekel tax paid for."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-7-218", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-25-kings-toll-tribute", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: Vespasian 'laid a tribute upon the Jews wheresoever they were, and enjoined every one of them to bring two drachmae every year into the Capitol' - a king taxing a subject people, the practice Jesus' saying describes (post-70 CE, the tax later called the Fiscus Judaicus)."}
{"from": "artifact:tyrian-shekel", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-27-stater-fish", "grade": "C", "note": "The stater 'was reckoned as equal to four drachmæ, and would therefore pay the didrachma both for Peter and his Master' - the Tyrian tetradrachm ('shekel') is the coin of this value in which the tax was paid; one coin covers two half-shekel dues."}
{"from": "observation:galilee-fish-species", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-17-27-stater-fish", "grade": "C", "note": "The lake's catch includes musht (tilapia) among four native fish types - the fish Peter could hook; which species carried the coin the text does not say (reused from ch 13/14)."}
